      "components": ["Module1", "U21", "U20"],
      "pins": ["Module1.55", "Module1.78", "U21.4", "U21.15", "U20.2"],
      "nets": ["TX_GPIO14"],
      "observed": "The net named TX_GPIO14 contains both the 3.3 V power rail and the UART TX signal path: it includes Module1.55 (GPIO14), U21.4 (RXD), U21.15 (VCC), U21.3 (VCCIO), U21.13 (3V3OUT), U20.2 (LDO VO), and Module1.78 (GPIO_VREF). Thus GPIO14 and the FT231 RXD input are tied directly to the 3.3 V regulator output rail.",
      "expected": "GPIO14/TX should be a separate signal net connected to U21 RXD, while the 3.3 V regulator output and GPIO_VREF should remain a power net. The companion UART path RX_GPIO15 is kept separate, showing the intended TX/RX pair should be distinct signal nets.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "TX_GPIO14 : ... Module1.55(GPIO14,passive) Module1.78(GPIO_VREF(1.8v/3.3v_Input),power_in) ... U20.2(VO,power_out) ... U21.4(RXD,input) ... U21.15(VCC,power_in)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "RX_GPIO15 : J4.2(Pin_2,passive) Module1.51(GPIO15,passive) U21.20(TXD,output)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The UART TX line is held at 3.3 V and the FT231 RXD input is tied to its supply, preventing normal serial communication. If GPIO14 is configured as an output and driven low, it directly shorts the 3.3 V regulator output to ground through the Pi GPIO.",
      "confidence": 0.82
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist shows a clear bridged-net anomaly: the TX_GPIO14 net merges the 3.3 V power rail with the GPIO14/FT231 UART TX signal, which would make the serial path behave incorrectly and could cause a direct short if GPIO14 is driven low."
}

